<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Theobald Bourke, 1st Viscount Mayo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1567–<a href="/years/1629">1629</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Tibbot na Long · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-burgh">House of Burgh</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Theobald Bourke, known as Tibbot na Long ('of the ships'), was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grace-omalley">Grace O'Malley</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-an-iarainn-bourke">Richard an Iarainn Bourke</a>, said to have been born at sea. He grew up as a Mayo lord and at first fought the English, but sided with the Crown during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-years-war-ireland">Nine Years' War</a>, including at Kinsale in 1601. He was knighted and in 1627 created Viscount Mayo by Charles I.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tibbot belonged to the MacWilliam Íochtar, the Lower MacWilliam Burkes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-mayo">County Mayo</a>, a Hiberno-Norman lineage descended from William de Burgh, who had been granted the overlordship of Connacht in 1215. Tradition says he was born just before his mother's fleet fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbary-coast">Barbary</a> pirates. By 1585 Gráinne O'Malley was ruling the lordship with him, and that year the lord deputy, Sir John Perrott, took him hostage to secure her compliance with the Composition of Connaught; as a prisoner he learnt English, and he married Maeve O'Conor Sligo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Freed by Richard Bingham in 1586, Tibbot joined a rebellion, sought a truce in 1587, rose again in 1589 and made peace in March 1590, by then the recognised chief of his clan. In 1592 he raised Mayo for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-hugh-odonnell">Red Hugh O'Donnell</a>, but was left without support when O'Donnell sought peace, and was pardoned. In May 1593 he was arrested for promising to raise an army in support of Brian Óg O'Rourke; his mother visited Queen Elizabeth in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in June and obtained his release, and on a second visit in April 1594 she secured favourable terms of surrender and regrant for him.</p><p>In 1595 O'Donnell had a cousin, Tibbot MacWalter Kittagh Bourke, made chief in his place, and Tibbot would not join O'Donnell and Hugh O'Neill in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-years-war-ireland">Nine Years' War</a>. He first helped the English commander Clifford, then took a less active part. Knighted the year after James I succeeded Elizabeth in 1603, he was arrested in 1608 on a charge of plotting to help the earls who had fled Ireland, which proved unfounded, and in 1610 he was again suspected and pardoned. He came to hold most of the lands of the smaller clans he represented in their dealings with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1613–15 he sat as one of the two members for Mayo in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-ireland">Parliament of Ireland</a>, voting as a Catholic against new boroughs for Protestant members. Cleared with his son Miles of plotting a Spanish-supported Catholic revolt during the Anglo-Spanish War, he was made viscount in June 1627, and in 1628 joined other Catholic nobles in petitioning for the reforms known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charites">Graces</a>. He died on 18 June 1629 and was buried at Ballintubber Abbey. Miles, raised as an Anglican and educated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, succeeded him, and the air Lord Mayo is said to have been composed by David Murphy to win back his favour.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Theobald Bourke, 1st Viscount Mayo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Burke rebellions, <b>Theobald Burke</b>, 'Tibbot na Long', son of Grace O'Malley, sided with the crown and was made Viscount Mayo in 1627.</blockquote><a href="/state/mac-william-iochtar">Mac William Iochtar</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-mayo">County Mayo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.9,-9.25&amp;year=1607" aria-label="County Mayo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4345 313 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Theobald Bourke, 1st Viscount Mayo"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4795 583h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4795 583h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=53.9,-9.25&amp;year=1607">What was here in 1607? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
